msgid "Custom HttpSecurityPolicy"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can register your own named policy by creating an application-scoped CDI "
"bean that implements the `io.quarkus.vertx.http.runtime.security."
"HttpSecurityPolicy` interface, as shown in the following example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Named HTTP Security policy will only be applied to requests matched by the "
"`application.properties` path matching rules."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Custom policy name must match the value returned by the `io.quarkus.vertx."
"http.runtime.security.HttpSecurityPolicy.name` method."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can bind custom named HttpSecurityPolicy to Jakarta REST "
"endpoints with the `@AuthorizationPolicy` security annotation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `@AuthorizationPolicy` annotation can be used together with other "
"standard security annotations.\n"
"As usual, the method-level annotation has priority over class-level "
"annotation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Apply custom named HttpSecurityPolicy to the Jakarta REST `hello` endpoint."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also create a global `HttpSecurityPolicy` invoked on every request.\n"
"Just do not implement the `io.quarkus.vertx.http.runtime.security."
"HttpSecurityPolicy.name` method and leave the policy nameless."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Inject <code>@RequestScoped</code> beans into <code>HttpSecurityPolicy</"
"code>"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@RequestScoped` beans can only be injected when the xref:cdi-reference."
"adoc#request-context-lifecycle[CDI request context] is active.\n"
"Users can activate the context, for example, with the "
"`@ActivateRequestContext` annotation.\n"
"However, authorization happens before Quarkus prepares some `@RequestScoped` "
"beans.\n"
"Let Quarkus activate and prepare CDI request context for you.\n"
"For example, consider a situation where you want to inject a bean from the "
"Jakarta REST context, such as the `jakarta.ws.rs.core.UriInfo` bean.\n"
"In this case, you must apply the `HttpSecurityPolicy` to Jakarta REST "
"endpoints.\n"
"You can use one of the following methods:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the `@AuthorizationPolicy` security annotation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Set the `quarkus.http.auth.permission.custom1.applies-to=jaxrs` "
"configuration property."
msgstr ""

msgid "Matching on paths and methods"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Permission sets can also specify paths and methods as a comma-separated list."
"\n"
"If a path ends with the `*` wildcard, the query it generates matches all sub-"
"paths.\n"
"Otherwise, it queries for an exact match and only matches that specific path:"
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `$$*$$` wildcard at the end of the path matches zero or more path "
"segments, but never any word starting from the `/public` path.\n"
"For that reason, a path like `/public-info` is not matched by this pattern."
msgstr ""

msgid "Matching a path but not a method"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The request is rejected if it matches one or more permission sets based on "
"the path but none of the required methods."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Given the preceding permission set, `GET /public/foo` would match both the "
"path and method and therefore be allowed.\n"
"In contrast, `POST /public/foo` would match the path but not the method, "
"and, therefore, be rejected."
msgstr ""

msgid "Matching multiple paths: longest path wins"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Matching is always done on the \"longest path wins\" basis.\n"
"Less specific permission sets are not considered if a more specific one has "
"been matched:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Given the preceding permission set, `GET /public/forbidden-folder/foo` would "
"match both permission sets' paths.\n"
"However, because the longer path matches the path of the `deny1` permission "
"set, `deny1` is chosen, and the request is rejected."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Subpath permissions precede root path permissions, as the `deny1` versus "
"`permit1` permission example previously illustrated."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This rule is further exemplified by a scenario where subpath permission "
"allows access to a public resource while the root path permission "
"necessitates authorization."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Matching multiple sub-paths: longest path to the <code>*</code> wildcard "
"wins"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Previous examples demonstrated matching all sub-paths when a path concludes "
"with the `$$*$$`\n"
"wildcard."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This wildcard can also be applied in the middle of a path, representing a "
"single path segment.\n"
"It cannot be mixed with other path segment characters; thus, path separators "
"always enclose the `$$*$$` wildcard, as seen in the `/public/$$*$$/about-us` "
"path."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When several path patterns correspond to the same request path, the system "
"selects the longest sub-path leading to the `$$*$$` wildcard.\n"
"In this context, every path segment character is more specific than the "
"`$$*$$`\n"
"wildcard."
msgstr ""

msgid "Here is a simple example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Request paths like `/api/product/detail` can only be accessed by "
"authenticated users."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The path `/api/public-product/detail` is more specific, therefore accessible "
"by anyone."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All paths secured with the authorization using configuration should be "
"tested.\n"
"Writing path patterns with multiple wildcards can be cumbersome.\n"
"Please make sure paths are authorized as you intended."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the following example, paths are ordered from the most specific to the "
"least specific one:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `$$*$$` wildcard at the end of the path matches zero or more path "
"segments.\n"
"The `$$*$$` wildcard placed anywhere else matches exactly one path segment."
msgstr ""

msgid "Matching multiple paths: most specific method wins"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When a path is registered with multiple permission sets, the permission sets "
"explicitly specifying an HTTP method that matches the request take "
"precedence.\n"
"In this instance, the permission sets without methods only come into effect "
"if the request method does not match permission sets with the method "
"specification."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The preceding permission set shows that `GET /public/foo` matches the paths "
"of both permission sets.\n"
"However, it specifically aligns with the explicit method of the `permit1` "
"permission set.\n"
"Therefore, `permit1` is selected, and the request is accepted."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In contrast, `PUT /public/foo` does not match the method permissions of "
"`permit1`.\n"
"As a result, `deny1` is activated, leading to the rejection of the request."
msgstr ""

msgid "Matching multiple paths and methods: both win"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Sometimes, the previously described rules allow multiple permission sets to "
"win simultaneously.\n"
"In that case, for the request to proceed, all the permissions must allow "
"access.\n"
"For this to happen, both must either have specified the method or have no "
"method.\n"
"Method-specific matches take precedence."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuration properties to deny access"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following configuration settings alter the role-based access control "
"(RBAC) denying behavior:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.security.jaxrs.deny-unannotated-endpoints=true|false`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If set to true, access is denied for all Jakarta REST endpoints by default.\n"
"If a Jakarta REST endpoint has no security annotations, it defaults to the "
"`@DenyAll` behavior.\n"
"This helps you to avoid accidentally exposing an endpoint that is supposed "
"to be secured.\n"
"Defaults to `false`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.security.jaxrs.default-roles-allowed=role1,role2`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Defines the default role requirements for unannotated endpoints.\n"
"The `**` role is a special role that means any authenticated user.\n"
"This cannot be combined with `deny-unannotated-endpoints` because `deny` "
"takes effect instead."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.security.deny-unannotated-members=true|false`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If set to true, the access is denied to all CDI methods and Jakarta REST "
"endpoints that do not have security annotations but are defined in classes "
"that contain methods with security annotations.\n"
"Defaults to `false`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disabling permissions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Permissions can be disabled at build time with an `enabled` property for "
"each declared permission, such as:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Permissions can be reenabled at runtime with a system property or "
"environment variable, such as:\n"
"`-Dquarkus.http.auth.permission.permit1.enabled=true`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Permission paths and HTTP root path"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus.http.root-path` configuration property changes the xref:http-"
"reference.adoc#context-path[http endpoint context path]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, `quarkus.http.root-path` is prepended automatically to "
"configured permission paths then do not use a forward slash, for example:"
msgstr ""

msgid "This configuration is equivalent to the following:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A leading slash changes how the configured permission path is interpreted.\n"
"The configured URL is used as-is, and paths are not adjusted if the value of "
"`quarkus.http.root-path` changes."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This configuration only impacts resources served from the fixed or static "
"URL, `/public`, which might not match your application resources if `quarkus."
"http.root-path` has been set to something other than `/`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For more information, see link:https://quarkus.io/blog/path-resolution-in-"
"quarkus/[Path Resolution in Quarkus]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Map <code>SecurityIdentity</code> roles"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The winning role-based policy that was chosen to authorize the current "
"request can map `SecurityIdentity` roles to deployment-specific roles.\n"
"These roles are then applicable for endpoint authorization by using the "
"`@RolesAllowed` annotation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Map the `admin` role to the `Admin1` role.\n"
"The `SecurityIdentity` will have both `admin` and `Admin1` roles."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `/*` path is secured, and only authenticated HTTP requests are granted "
"access."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If all that you need is to map the `SecurityIdentity` roles to the "
"deployment-specific roles regardless of a path, you can also do this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `/*` path is not secured.\n"
"You must secure your endpoints with standard security annotations or define "
"HTTP permissions in addition to this configuration property."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you prefer a programmatic configuration, the same mapping can be added "
"with the `io.quarkus.vertx.http.security.HttpSecurity` CDI event:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Shared permission checks"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One important rule for unshared permission checks is that only one path "
"match is applied, the most specific one.\n"
"When a path matches as the most specific, you can specify multiple "
"permissions for that path and they are all applied.\n"
"However, there can be permission checks you want to apply to many paths "
"without repeating them over and over again.\n"
"That's where shared permission checks come in, they are always applied when "
"the permission path is matched."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<<custom-http-security-policy>> will be also applied on the `/admin/1` path "
"together with the `admin-policy1` policy."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Role `root` will be able to access `/secured/user/\\*` and `/secured/admin/"
"*` paths."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `/secured/*` path can only be accessed by authenticated users.\n"
"This way, you have secured the `/secured/all` path and so on."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Shared permissions are always applied before unshared ones, therefore a "
"`SecurityIdentity` with the `root` role will have the `user` role as well."
msgstr ""

